```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: leaf_router_0_0, leaf_router_0_1
diagnosis_text:
The eBGP ECMP configuration is absent from both leaf routers, completely
disabling the load-balancing that is the core design goal of the Clos fabric.

Both leaf_router_0_0 (AS 65200) and leaf_router_0_1 (AS 65201) have two BGP
sessions — one to spine_router_0_0 (AS 65100) and one to spine_router_0_1
(AS 65101) — and they each receive the remote host subnet (10.0.1.0/24 on
leaf_router_0_0; 10.0.0.0/24 on leaf_router_0_1) from both spines. The BGP RIB
on leaf_router_0_0 shows exactly two paths for 10.0.1.0/24: one via 172.16.0.4
(spine_router_0_0, AS-path 65100 65201, marked best) and one via 172.16.0.8
(spine_router_0_1, AS-path 65101 65201, marked valid but not installed). Because
neither leaf router has `maximum-paths` configured in its BGP address-family, and
because the two paths differ in their first transit AS (65100 vs 65101), which
requires `bgp bestpath as-path multipath-relax` to be treated as equal-cost, only
the single router-ID-preferred path via spine_router_0_0 is ever installed in the
FIB. spine_router_0_1 carries zero leaf-to-leaf traffic.

Current end-to-end connectivity between pc_0_0 and pc_0_1 works (5/5 pings
succeed) because the one installed path is functional — the team's claim that the
hosts "cannot reach" each other is incorrect. However, ECMP is completely broken
and the fabric has no redundancy: a failure of spine_router_0_0 would black-hole
all inter-host traffic until BGP reconverges.

Root cause: `maximum-paths 2` and `bgp bestpath as-path multipath-relax` are
missing from the BGP configuration of leaf_router_0_0 and leaf_router_0_1.

Fix: On both leaf routers, add `bgp bestpath as-path multipath-relax` under
`router bgp <ASN>` and add `maximum-paths 2` under `address-family ipv4 unicast`,
then soft-clear the BGP sessions (`clear ip bgp * soft`) to re-evaluate paths.
=== END ===
```